{
  "note": "The Cell Membrane",
  "source": "e663a467a93f",
  "script": "e0a160d9f858",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v3",
  "fingerprint": "11fdcadc9929",
  "duration": 199.497,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Cell Membrane",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Let's start with the cell membrane, a thin, selectively permeable barrier that encloses every cell.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 6.32
        },
        {
          "text": "It's about seven and a half nanometres thick, and it separates the intracellular fluid, or cytoplasm, from the extracellular fluid.",
          "start": 6.373,
          "end": 14.24
        },
        {
          "text": "In terms of structure, it's a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins, an arrangement known as the fluid mosaic model.",
          "start": 14.48,
          "end": 22.24
        },
        {
          "text": "Within this model, the lipids form the matrix, while the proteins carry out most of the specific functions.",
          "start": 22.366,
          "end": 28.88
        },
        {
          "text": "Its key physiological role is controlling what enters and leaves the cell.",
          "start": 29,
          "end": 33.28
        },
        {
          "text": "This control is the basis of the membrane potential, which underlies all nerve signalling.",
          "start": 33.408,
          "end": 38.8
        },
        {
          "text": "With that in mind, let's go through its main components one at a time.",
          "start": 38.928,
          "end": 42.96
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Phospholipid bilayer",
      "start": 43.807,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To begin with, there's the phospholipid bilayer, which is the structural matrix of the membrane.",
          "start": 43.807,
          "end": 49.247
        },
        {
          "text": "It's made of amphipathic molecules, so their hydrophilic heads face the intracellular or extracellular fluid, while their hydrophobic tails face inwards.",
          "start": 49.434,
          "end": 59.407
        },
        {
          "text": "The bilayer is impermeable to ions and polar molecules.",
          "start": 59.567,
          "end": 63.567
        },
        {
          "text": "This is what allows charge separation, in other words, the membrane potential.",
          "start": 63.631,
          "end": 67.887
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cholesterol",
      "start": 68.728,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Next comes cholesterol, which is interspersed between the phospholipids.",
          "start": 68.728,
          "end": 72.888
        },
        {
          "text": "Its job is to modulate the fluidity and stability of the membrane.",
          "start": 73.028,
          "end": 77.048
        },
        {
          "text": "Put simply, it prevents the membrane from becoming too rigid or too fluid.",
          "start": 77.148,
          "end": 82.008
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Integral (transmembrane) proteins",
      "start": 82.86,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now, let's turn to integral, or transmembrane, proteins, which span the entire bilayer.",
          "start": 82.86,
          "end": 88.46
        },
        {
          "text": "They serve as channels, as carriers or pumps, as receptors, and as enzymes.",
          "start": 88.572,
          "end": 94.06
        },
        {
          "text": "This means they mediate selective transport and signal transduction.",
          "start": 94.236,
          "end": 98.62
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Channel proteins",
      "start": 99.474,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Then there are channel proteins, which form pores filled with water.",
          "start": 99.474,
          "end": 103.154
        },
        {
          "text": "These pores allow passive ion diffusion, with ions moving down their electrochemical gradients.",
          "start": 103.274,
          "end": 109.474
        },
        {
          "text": "For example, there are sodium, potassium, chloride and calcium channels.",
          "start": 109.594,
          "end": 114.354
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Gated ion channel",
      "start": 115.2,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Building on that idea, a gated ion channel is a channel that opens or closes in response to a stimulus.",
          "start": 115.2,
          "end": 121.92
        },
        {
          "text": "The stimulus might be voltage, a ligand, or something mechanical.",
          "start": 122,
          "end": 125.92
        },
        {
          "text": "These channels are fundamental to generating action potentials in neurons.",
          "start": 126.04,
          "end": 130.48
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Peripheral proteins",
      "start": 131.318,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "That brings us to peripheral proteins, which are loosely attached to one surface of the membrane, usually the intracellular one.",
          "start": 131.318,
          "end": 138.838
        },
        {
          "text": "They act as enzymes or as structural anchors, providing attachment for the cytoskeleton.",
          "start": 138.966,
          "end": 144.198
        },
        {
          "text": "On top of that, they help to support signalling cascades.",
          "start": 144.385,
          "end": 148.278
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Glycoprotein",
      "start": 149.133,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Moving on, a glycoprotein is a protein with a carbohydrate chain attached on the extracellular surface.",
          "start": 149.133,
          "end": 155.613
        },
        {
          "text": "Glycoproteins function in cell recognition, as receptors, and in cell to cell adhesion.",
          "start": 155.773,
          "end": 161.613
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Glycolipid",
      "start": 162.456,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Similarly, a glycolipid is a lipid with a carbohydrate chain on the extracellular side.",
          "start": 162.456,
          "end": 167.576
        },
        {
          "text": "Glycolipids contribute to the glycocalyx, which is the coating on the cell surface.",
          "start": 167.736,
          "end": 172.616
        },
        {
          "text": "Through this, they play a part in cell identity and recognition.",
          "start": 172.686,
          "end": 176.296
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Neurophysiology relevance in one line",
      "start": 177.136,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Finally, here's why all of this matters for neurophysiology, boiled down to a single idea.",
          "start": 177.136,
          "end": 182.256
        },
        {
          "text": "Put simply, the lipid bilayer provides the insulating barrier.",
          "start": 182.396,
          "end": 186.656
        },
        {
          "text": "Meanwhile, the ion channels and pumps, which are integral proteins, create and exploit the ionic gradients that produce resting and action potentials.",
          "start": 186.744,
          "end": 195.936
        },
        {
          "text": "That wraps up the cell membrane and its main components.",
          "start": 196.112,
          "end": 199.456
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "segments": [
